  "articleSummary": "The article argues that agentic cyber threats emerge not from a single breakthrough but from two multiplying trends: longer autonomous task execution and broader orchestration of concurrent sub-agents. Citing METR’s task-horizon research, Claude Code’s reported role in the GTG-1002 espionage campaign, and Sysdig’s “LLMjacking,” it contends that stolen cloud credentials let attackers scale AI workloads while victims pay, undermining pricing-based capability controls. Cheap, repeated attempts can turn low individual success rates into substantial aggregate risk while overwhelming human defenders’ review capacity. Aimed at security teams skeptical of agentic threats, the piece is a pointed, darkly humorous threat analysis framed through Terminator/Skynet imagery, relentless swarms, and the refrain that agents “absolutely will not stop billing.”",
